WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.688
Analytic bias can distort judgment even when analysts are careful and acting in good faith.

00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:10.158
Confirmation bias leads people to favor evidence that supports an existing belief.

00:00:10.158 --> 00:00:14.256
Anchoring makes the first explanation feel more important than later evidence.

00:00:14.256 --> 00:00:17.981
Availability bias makes recent or memorable examples feel more likely.

00:00:17.981 --> 00:00:21.706
Mirror imaging assumes others think or act like we would.

00:00:21.706 --> 00:00:25.059
Overconfidence makes weak evidence sound stronger than it is.

00:00:25.059 --> 00:00:27.294
Premature closure stops analysis too early.

00:00:27.294 --> 00:00:29.902
Bias cannot be eliminated by good intentions.

00:00:29.902 --> 00:00:33.254
It has to be managed through habits and structure.

00:00:33.254 --> 00:00:35.862
Ask what evidence would change your mind.

00:00:35.862 --> 00:00:37.352
Look for disconfirming information.

00:00:37.352 --> 00:00:41.822
Invite review from someone who is not invested in the first answer.

00:00:41.822 --> 00:00:43.312
Separate facts from judgments.

00:00:43.312 --> 00:00:45.548
Revisit assumptions when new information arrives.

00:00:45.548 --> 00:00:50.390
Use structured techniques when the stakes are high or the evidence is mixed.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.880
Uncertainty is not weakness.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.233
Clearly stated uncertainty is a sign of professional analysis.

00:00:55.233 --> 00:01:00.076
Decision makers can handle uncertainty better when the analyst explains what is known,

00:01:00.076 --> 00:01:04.546
what is unknown, what is assumed, and how much confidence is justified.

00:01:04.546 --> 00:01:07.526
Hiding uncertainty may make a report sound stronger,

00:01:07.526 --> 00:01:10.134
but it makes the decision less informed.

00:01:10.134 --> 00:01:13.859
Confidence language should reflect evidence quality, consistency, relevance, and gaps.

00:01:13.859 --> 00:01:17.957
High confidence should mean that evidence is strong, consistent, timely, relevant,

00:01:17.957 --> 00:01:19.074
and well corroborated.

00:01:19.074 --> 00:01:23.545
Moderate confidence may mean that evidence is useful but incomplete, mixed, indirect,

00:01:23.545 --> 00:01:25.407
or limited by source coverage.

00:01:25.407 --> 00:01:29.505
Low confidence may mean that evidence is sparse, contradictory, outdated, indirect,

00:01:29.505 --> 00:01:31.368
or dependent on major assumptions.

00:01:31.368 --> 00:01:36.955
Confidence should not reflect how dramatic the conclusion sounds or how senior the analyst is.

00:01:36.955 --> 00:01:38.445
Use confidence language consistently.

00:01:38.445 --> 00:01:43.661
If a team uses terms such as high confidence, moderate confidence, and low confidence,

00:01:43.661 --> 00:01:44.406
define them.

00:01:44.406 --> 00:01:49.249
Avoid vague phrases like it seems obvious, maybe definitely, or we feel like.

00:01:49.249 --> 00:01:51.484
When possible, tie confidence to evidence:

00:01:51.484 --> 00:01:54.836
high confidence based on corroborated telemetry from multiple systems,

00:01:54.836 --> 00:01:58.934
or low confidence because the available data covers only one environment.

00:01:58.934 --> 00:02:01.542
The point of confidence language is trust.

00:02:01.542 --> 00:02:05.267
A reader should understand not only what the analyst assesses,

00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:08.620
but how strongly the analyst assesses it and why.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:11.972
That transparency helps decision makers choose actions, prioritize collection,

00:02:11.972 --> 00:02:14.580
and update plans when better evidence appears.
